{
  "id": "Compound::DB00748 - Gene::1565",
  "verbalization": "Carbinoxamine is a histamine H1 receptor antagonist that is included in the class of compounds that includes chlorphenamine and diphenhydramine. These compounds bind to Cyp2d6, a cytochrome P450 enzyme involved in drug metabolism. The evidence suggests that Carbinoxamine, through its inclusion in the histamine H1 receptor antagonist class, may interact with Cyp2d6, potentially influencing its activity. The lowest common ancestor of Carbinoxamine and its related compounds points to the broader category of monochlorobenzenes, which may provide a structural basis for the interaction. The evidence from multiple paths indicates that Carbinoxamine may act as a modulator of Cyp2d6, possibly through a shared mechanism involving histamine receptor binding, which could explain its potential role in drug metabolism. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of histamine receptor antagonists in the regulation of Cyp2d6 activity, suggesting a possible dependency on these compounds in metabolic processes.",
